The vocabulary of this account
29c-sd-tcl-the-development-tools
The list shows every entry of the VOC, the file that makes a command line work: its type (V a verb, K a keyword, F a file, Q a pointer to a file in another account, R a pointer to a record, PA a paragraph, S a stored sentence, X text), what it points at, whether a pointer works (the file it names is opened to see), and how it stands against NEWVOC, the shipped vocabulary every account was copied from: the same, changed, or added in this account. The box under the list shows the entry under the cursor field by field.
E edits the entry in the IDE (the terminal is handed to the IDE, which locks the entry while it is open, and taken back); the page itself writes nothing. D deletes the entry after a box that shows the exact command and the id typed. T chooses which type is listed, S which state (all, differing from the shipped VOC, broken pointers), / or F11 filters by a part of the id, Esc shows every entry.
The gates line shows the edit gates your session holds. Writing the VOC by hand needs voc-edit: without it the IDE's save and the delete are refused by the kernel and the entry is left as it was. A new pointer made by an ordinary command such as create.file is not held back.
